+# 4. Security Standards for the Government Platform
+
+Security controls must be risk-based, fail closed, and supported by evidence. This rule is an engineering baseline; it does not establish legal certification or an approved information-system security level.
+
+## 4.1. Authentication trust boundary
+
+- Maintain exactly one approved RS256 private-key holder and token issuer.
+- In the current source, `user-service` verifies credentials and owns refresh/session/auth-version state; `api-gateway` signs RS256 access and internal delegation tokens. A boundary change requires an accepted ADR, coordinated migration, rotation, rollback, and contract tests.
+- No other service may hold the private key or add an HS256/shared-secret fallback.
+- Validators must pin the allowed algorithm, trusted key and `kid`, exact issuer, intended audience, signature, expiry, subject/session identifiers, and required revocation/auth-version state.
+- Unsigned identity headers or gRPC metadata are untrusted. Receiving services must validate trusted signed/asserted context and still enforce resource-level authorization.
+
+## 4.2. Authorization and separation of duties
+
+- PBAC is authoritative. Evaluate subject, action, resource, context, organization scope, and data scope; never infer permission from a role name.
+- Gateway checks do not replace authorization in the owning service. Frontend checks are UX only and never grant authority.
+- Fail closed for authentication, authorization, approval, publication, signing, classified data, and unavailable policy/session state.
+- Apply approved separation of duties for administration, approval, publication, signing, audit review, and exception approval.
+
+## 4.3. Data protection, records, and audit
+
+- Classify data and apply minimization, masking, encryption, access, retention, legal-hold, and disposal controls under the approved policy.
+- Audit security-sensitive actions and state changes with actor, action, resource, organization scope, decision/outcome, timestamp, correlation ID, and trusted source context.
+- Store an approved, masked field-level change summary or integrity reference. Never log passwords, tokens, private keys, secrets, unrestricted request payloads, or unnecessary personal data.
+- Protect audit storage against unauthorized alteration/deletion and verify access, retention, export, and recovery controls operationally.
+- Soft delete is not a retention policy. Preserve or dispose of records according to the approved schedule and audited process.
+
+## 4.4. Application and integration security
+
+- Validate input at every trust boundary; use parameterized queries and explicit response DTOs. Enforce resource ownership and organization/data scope to prevent IDOR.
+- Preserve source content when required for administrative/legal records. For rendered active content, use approved allow-list sanitization plus contextual output encoding.
+- External destinations must come from a server-controlled registry with protocol, host/address, DNS/redirect, method/path, timeout, response-size, and record-count restrictions.
+- Never forward browser cookies, Hub tokens, identity headers, or secrets to an external upstream. Use server-side secret references and redacted integration logs.
+- Encrypt external traffic and internal channels according to the approved deployment design. Configure trusted proxies explicitly; never trust arbitrary forwarding headers.
+
+## 4.5. Secrets and production configuration
+
+- Never hard-code or log credentials, tokens, private keys, or API secrets.
+- Repository demo/default credentials are non-production examples. Production must fail fast when required secrets are absent; do not use known fallback secrets.
+- If a default or exposed value may have reached an environment, rotate it and record evidence rather than only changing documentation.
+- Store keys in an approved secret facility and maintain documented generation, rotation, overlap, revocation, backup, and rollback procedures.
+
+## 4.6. Signatures and integrity
+
+A SHA-256 hash alone is not a digital signature or proof of non-repudiation. A legally significant signing flow requires the approved signing service/PKI, certificate-chain and revocation validation, trusted time evidence, signer authorization, and retained verification evidence.
